2186268882 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09f5edfe-2ef7-5aa3-b9a3-3d13ae59dd49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268882 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268887 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268887 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268887 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268887 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268887 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268887 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186321176 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186321176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0315c355-94e8-5b85-bc0d-c26e707e74cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321182 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0.5 \\\\ z &=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186321182 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321182 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03a88646-da7b-5cc5-a6ca-e3694eb78578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321387 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=4y+8\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186321387 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321387 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "204035e4-46d4-534d-ba05-15ce0e052f85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321526 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186321526 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321526 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31748a7a-7455-5dc9-aebc-92650c97c472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321611 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321611 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863